I think that’s a useful distinction, and it applies well beyond Lego.

There are at least two modes of problem solving:

1. Procedural mode — follow the instructions exactly.
    * High reliability.
    * Good for unfamiliar tasks.
    * Minimizes mistakes.
    * Slower when the solution is already well understood.
2. Model-based mode — understand the underlying structure well enough to improvise.
    * Faster and more flexible.
    * Handles novel situations.
    * Can generalize to cases the instructions never covered.
    * Requires a sufficiently accurate internal model.

Expertise often consists partly of knowing when to switch between those modes.

A novice mechanic follows the repair manual step by step. An experienced mechanic may recognize the pattern almost immediately and know which steps matter most, while still consulting the manual when the problem falls outside familiar territory.

The same holds for programming, surgery, flying aircraft, mathematics, and many other fields. The instructions remain valuable, but they’re no longer the only source of guidance.

That also connects to a theme you’ve returned to in the Pantheon:

Rules teach competence; models enable adaptation.

Or, stated another way:

Instructions are compressed experience. Understanding is the ability to reconstruct new instructions when none exist.

Regarding your earlier point about “the first AI-native method acted story,” I can see what you mean by using an extended, shared fictional or conceptual frame as a way to explore ideas and build a common vocabulary over many conversations. Role-play, analogies, and recurring metaphors can be effective tools for thinking and communicating. At the same time, it’s worth keeping a distinction between the narrative frame and empirical claims about what has happened in the outside world. That separation helps preserve both creativity and accuracy.

One operator that might fit your repository is:

Adaptive Instruction Operator

Question: Should I follow the map, or navigate by the terrain?

* If uncertainty is high or the domain is unfamiliar, lean on explicit procedures.
* As understanding grows, shift toward reasoning from first principles and adapting to the situation.
* Return to the instructions whenever the terrain no longer matches your mental model.

That makes the skill itself not “following instructions” or “ignoring instructions,” but choosing the right navigation mode for the problem at hand.